📚 Stack  Summary Notes

 ✅ Definition

A Stack is a linear data structure that follows the LIFO (Last In First Out) principle.
The element added last is removed first.

 🧱 Core Operations

| Operation   | Description                            | Time Complexity |
|  |  |  |
| push(x)   | Add element x to the top             | O(1)            |
| pop()     | Remove and return the top element      | O(1)            |
| peek()    | Return top element without removing it | O(1)            |
| isEmpty() | Check if stack is empty                | O(1)            |
| size()    | Return number of elements in stack     | O(1)            |

 🧰 Implementation Options

 1. Array or ArrayList
 Fast access, but fixed capacity unless using dynamic array
 Easy to implement

 2. Linked List
 Dynamic size
 More memory (due to node pointers)

 3. Builtin Libraries
 Java: Stack, or better: Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
 Python: list, or collections.deque
 C++: std::stack

 📦 Realworld Analogies

 Stack of plates/books
 Undo/redo functionality
 Browser back navigation
 Recursion call stack

 🧠 Common Use Cases

 Balanced parentheses, brackets {[()]}
 Expression evaluation (postfix, infix → postfix)
 Backtracking algorithms (Sudoku, Maze)
 Depthfirst search (DFS)
 Implementing undo/redo, history
 Stock span / next greater element problems

 🔍 Stack in Interviews

 Asked as:
 Direct stack implementation questions
 Problems that implicitly require a stack (e.g. "next greater element", "min stack")
 Reverse Polish Notation, evaluating expressions
 Simulating recursion (iterative DFS)

 🛠️ Popular Variants

 Min Stack: Supports getMin() in O(1)
 TwoStack Queue: Queue using two stacks
 Custom Stack with Capacity or frequency tracking

 🔄 Stack vs Queue

| Feature       | Stack (LIFO)        | Queue (FIFO)           |
|  |  |  |
| Insert/Remove | Top/Top             | Rear/Front             |
| Order         | Last in → First out | First in → First out   |
| Use Cases     | DFS, Undo, History  | BFS, Scheduling, Cache |
